1
00:00:00,435 --> 00:00:02,440
Seven hundred years before a Roman nail was

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,240
ever driven through a human wrist, a prophet

3
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,060
in Jerusalem wrote down the death of a man

4
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:07,820
he had never met.

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:12,540
He described the wounds, the silence, the grave

6
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:14,840
— and the rich man in his death.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,840
He even wrote down what the wounds were for.

8
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:20,960
And then he asked the question that still hangs

9
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:23,000
over the page: "Who hath believed our report?

10
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:25,500
and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed?"

11
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:26,840
This is Isaiah fifty-three.

12
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:29,860
Either it is the most astonishing accident

13
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:32,320
in all of human literature — or someone standing

14
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,700
outside of time was telling us, centuries early,

15
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:37,040
exactly how God would save the world.

16
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:39,520
It opens with God Himself pointing.

17
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:43,300
"Behold, my servant shall deal prudently, he

18
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,100
shall be exalted and extolled, and be very

19
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,740
high." You brace yourself for a conquering

20
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:49,040
king.

21
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:51,380
And then the picture breaks.

22
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:53,720
"…his visage was so marred more than any man,

23
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,440
and his form more than the sons of men…" A

24
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:57,900
face beaten past recognising.

25
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:00,940
"…he hath no form nor comeliness; and when

26
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:03,320
we shall see him, there is no beauty that we

27
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:04,040
should desire him.

28
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:07,700
He is despised and rejected of men; a man of

29
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,880
sorrows, and acquainted with grief…" God's

30
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,540
chosen Servant does not arrive in armour.

31
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,400
He arrives in agony.

32
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,780
The world looks at him — and looks away.

33
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:22,640
The exalted one and the despised one are the

34
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:23,240
same man.

35
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:26,740
Hold those two together — that tension is the

36
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:28,440
whole gospel in a single breath.

37
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:31,800
Then comes the centre of the chapter — and

38
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:33,280
every line is a transaction.

39
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,000
"Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried

40
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,580
our sorrows…" Our griefs.

41
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:38,960
Not his own.

42
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:40,860
"But he was wounded for our transgressions,

43
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:44,200
he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement

44
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,620
of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes

45
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:49,480
we are healed." Stay on the small words.

46
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:51,700
Our transgressions.

47
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:52,800
Our peace.

48
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:53,940
His wounds.

49
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:57,140
This is not a good man dying as a brave example.

50
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:00,680
This is a substitution: one stands in the place

51
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,280
of many and takes what they had earned.

52
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:04,860
And in case you think you are watching this

53
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:07,600
from a safe distance, Isaiah reaches out and

54
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,520
pulls you in: "All we like sheep have gone

55
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:11,880
astray; we have turned every one to his own

56
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:14,620
way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity

57
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:15,840
of us all." All of us.

58
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:16,860
Every one.

59
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:20,040
The straying is named — and then the rescue:

60
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:21,220
laid on him.

61
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:23,540
Then watch how he suffers.

62
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:26,200
"He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet

63
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,780
he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a

64
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:30,760
lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before

65
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:33,520
her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his

66
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:34,480
mouth." No defence.

67
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:36,960
No curse on his accusers.

68
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:38,340
Silent.

69
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,020
Now — an honest question.

70
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:43,500
For centuries many thoughtful Jewish readers

71
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:45,360
have understood this Servant not as one man,

72
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:48,660
but as the nation of Israel itself — a suffering

73
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,460
people, despised among the nations.

74
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:53,100
And they have a real text behind them.

75
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,160
Earlier in this same book, God calls the nation

76
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,260
by this very title: "…Thou art my servant,

77
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:01,140
O Israel, in whom I will be glorified." So

78
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,500
reading chapter fifty-three as Israel is not

79
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:03,880
a dodge.

80
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:05,840
It is the natural first guess.

81
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,920
It deserves a serious answer, not a sneer.

82
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:11,840
Here is what the chapter does that no nation

83
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:12,360
can survive.

84
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:16,240
First, the sufferer is sinless: "…he made his

85
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:18,140
grave with the wicked, and with the rich in

86
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:20,600
his death; because he had done no violence,

87
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,200
neither was any deceit in his mouth." No people

88
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:25,700
in history — not a nation, not even its most

89
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:28,240
faithful remnant — has ever been honestly described

90
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:28,740
that way.

91
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:32,100
Second, he dies for the people: "…for the transgression

92
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:34,060
of my people was he stricken." The Servant

93
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:36,600
stands in their place — so he cannot simply

94
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:37,180
be them.

95
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:39,280
A single grave.

96
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:40,780
A rich man's tomb.

97
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:42,440
A sinless life.

98
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,500
This is not a people.

99
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:47,060
This is a person.

100
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:50,120
Which leaves the only question that matters.

101
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:52,700
Who is he?

102
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,740
The New Testament records the exact moment

103
00:03:55,740 --> 00:03:56,940
someone asked it out loud.

104
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,800
A man from Ethiopia — a court official — is

105
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,700
riding home in his chariot, reading this very

106
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:03,880
chapter aloud: "…He was led as a sheep to the

107
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:05,980
slaughter; and like a lamb dumb before his

108
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:08,780
shearer, so opened he not his mouth…" Philip,

109
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:11,200
sent by the Spirit, comes alongside the chariot.

110
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:13,400
And the Ethiopian turns and asks the question

111
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,760
Isaiah seems to invite: "I pray thee, of whom

112
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:18,320
speaketh the prophet this?

113
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:22,520
of himself, or of some other man?" And Luke

114
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,740
gives the answer in one unforgettable line.

115
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,680
"Then Philip opened his mouth, and began at

116
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:28,780
the same scripture, and preached unto him Jesus."

117
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:31,080
He did not have to wrestle the text into shape.

118
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:33,700
He started at Isaiah fifty-three, and the road

119
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:35,260
led straight to Jesus of Nazareth.

120
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:37,740
The report Isaiah wondered if anyone would

121
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:40,980
believe — the New Testament answers: this is

122
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:41,380
the man.

123
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:44,040
But there is one line in this chapter heavier

124
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:45,100
than all the wounds.

125
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,560
"Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he

126
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:48,880
hath put him to grief…" Pleased.

127
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:52,780
Not because the Father delights in pain — but

128
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:54,580
because He delights in what the cross would

129
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:56,980
accomplish: a people brought home.

130
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,100
And the Servant is no victim caught in the

131
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,980
gears — Isaiah says he poured out his soul

132
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:03,640
unto death.

133
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:04,920
He gave it.

134
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:08,380
Then, quietly, the chapter turns toward morning.

135
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:11,000
"…he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his

136
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,320
days, and the pleasure of the LORD shall prosper

137
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:14,160
in his hand.

138
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:16,960
He shall see of the travail of his soul, and

139
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:19,180
shall be satisfied…" A man bruised to death

140
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:21,800
does not live to see his days prolonged — unless

141
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,020
death is not the end of him.

142
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:26,440
Isaiah does not spell out an empty tomb — but

143
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:28,320
he leaves a shape that only resurrection fills,

144
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,740
a shadow the New Testament would bring into

145
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:31,300
full light.

146
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:33,780
And the chapter's final note over the Servant

147
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:37,300
is not defeat but one word: satisfied.

148
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:39,440
The travail was not wasted.

149
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:40,720
It was enough.

150
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:43,480
So come back to where Isaiah began.

151
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:44,900
"Who hath believed our report?

152
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:47,040
and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed?"

153
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:49,560
The arm of the LORD — the saving strength of

154
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:51,160
God — is not a thing.

155
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:52,900
It is a person.

156
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:56,200
It has a face, marred more than any man's.

157
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,160
It has hands, wounded for transgressions that

158
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:00,020
were never his own.

159
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:03,000
And it has your name in verse six.

160
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:06,850
The straying it names — "we have turned every

161
00:06:06,850 --> 00:06:10,560
one to his own way" — is yours, and mine.

162
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:14,560
But read where the sentence ends: "the LORD

163
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:18,400
hath laid on him the iniquity of us all." Every

164
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,940
way you turned that was your own — laid on

165
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:21,240
him.

166
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:22,940
Already carried.

167
00:06:23,740 --> 00:06:24,820
Already taken away.

168
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:28,020
You do not have to clean yourself up before

169
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:28,420
you come.

170
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:31,180
That is the whole point of Isaiah fifty-three:

171
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:34,380
he was wounded for your transgressions, and

172
00:06:34,380 --> 00:06:35,460
bore them in your place.

173
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:37,500
The report is true.

174
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,020
The arm of the LORD has a name.

175
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:42,460
His name is Jesus.

176
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:45,020
Have you believed the report?
